WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.065 --> 00:00:16.236
Do you ever wonder who invented the light bulb, the movie camera, and the record player?

2
00:00:17.156 --> 00:00:20.059
Can you imagine your life without any of these items today?

3
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.643
The person we have to thank for all of them is Thomas Edison.

4
00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:30.508
He created hundreds of inventions in his lifetime that we continue to use today.

5
00:00:31.892 --> 00:00:36.136
Thomas Edison was born in Milan, Ohio, in 1847.

6
00:00:36.756 --> 00:00:39.539
He was the youngest in a household of seven children.

7
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.323
When he was young, Thomas was a very curious boy.

8
00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:47.846
He always asked questions about everything around him.

9
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.889
He wanted to know where things came from.

10
00:00:51.409 --> 00:00:53.711
His curiosity often got him in trouble.

11
00:00:54.652 --> 00:01:00.037
One day he asked his mother why the hen sat on her eggs, and she told him it

12
00:01:00.077 --> 00:01:02.259
was because the hen was hatching her eggs.

13
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.884
Without thinking, Thomas sat on a pile of eggs to try to hatch them too and broke them

14
00:01:09.065 --> 00:01:16.631
all. Another time he was curious about how fire worked, so he set his father's barn on fire

15
00:01:16.731 --> 00:01:17.792
to see what would happen.

16
00:01:18.753 --> 00:01:20.174
The barn burned down.

17
00:01:20.855 --> 00:01:26.259
His father was very angry with him and punished him by giving him a thrashing in the town

18
00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:35.182
square. When young Thomas started school, he was so curious that he disturbed his classmates by constantly interrupting

19
00:01:35.242 --> 00:01:36.582
the teacher with questions.

20
00:01:38.243 --> 00:01:41.924
For this reason Thomas was sent home to be taught by his mother.

21
00:01:42.904 --> 00:01:48.946
Thomas' mother understood her son's curiosity and patiently let his imagination grow.

22
00:01:49.926 --> 00:01:52.267
Thomas loved to conduct experiments.

23
00:01:53.247 --> 00:01:58.093
He set up a laboratory in his parents' attic and filled it with different chemicals.

24
00:01:59.094 --> 00:02:04.898
Strange smells, sounds, and even small explosions would often come out of the attic.

25
00:02:05.999 --> 00:02:08.200
Thomas wasn't homeschooled for very long.

26
00:02:09.001 --> 00:02:13.564
When he turned 16, he got a job as a train boy on the local train.

27
00:02:14.324 --> 00:02:19.168
He sold candy, newspapers, fruit, and sandwiches to the passengers.

28
00:02:20.248 --> 00:02:26.160
He loved his job because he got to set up a small laboratory in the baggage compartment, where

29
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.041
he was able to experiment during the long train rides.

30
00:02:30.522 --> 00:02:36.505
The conductor was not very happy about this, and after Thomas lit a small fire in the compartment,

31
00:02:36.945 --> 00:02:38.626
he was let go from his job.

32
00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:45.849
This did not faze the young inventor; he soon found a new passion—the telegraph machine.

33
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:51.852
In the 1860s there was a great need for telegraph operators throughout the United States.

34
00:02:52.768 --> 00:02:59.194
Thomas learned the telegraph code and was quickly hired, and worked as a telegraph operator for six years.

35
00:03:00.415 --> 00:03:03.858
But he was not content with the telegraph machine of the day.

36
00:03:04.839 --> 00:03:10.264
He wanted to improve it so that one machine could be used to both send and receive messages.

37
00:03:11.224 --> 00:03:15.668
He worked hard on his invention at night, often falling asleep at his desk.

38
00:03:16.869 --> 00:03:22.585
Finally he was able to improve the telegraph and sold the patent for his new machine to the

39
00:03:22.625 --> 00:03:23.946
Western Union company.

40
00:03:25.266 --> 00:03:32.309
Encouraged by the sale, and now with money to support his work, Thomas began experimenting on new inventions.

41
00:03:33.249 --> 00:03:36.771
He set up a small laboratory in Menlo Park, New Jersey.

42
00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:41.933
He was busy working on a new machine that would record sound.

43
00:03:42.753 --> 00:03:49.696
The telephone had recently been invented by Alexander Graham Bell, and Thomas was interested in this device.

44
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.741
He wanted to find ways to record the sounds that came out of the telephone.

45
00:03:56.162 --> 00:04:02.763
His new invention was called a phonograph and would become the prototype for the modern record player.

46
00:04:03.704 --> 00:04:05.504
It was an instant success.

47
00:04:06.024 --> 00:04:07.665
But Thomas did not stop there.

48
00:04:08.165 --> 00:04:11.666
His new passion became finding ways to use electricity.

49
00:04:12.766 --> 00:04:16.387
In those days people used oil lamps to light their homes.

50
00:04:17.187 --> 00:04:21.142
They were smoky and dangerous, and the light was not very strong.

51
00:04:23.123 --> 00:04:29.868
Thomas was convinced that by using electricity he could produce brighter, more uniform light that would be easier

52
00:04:29.908 --> 00:04:30.449
to use.

53
00:04:31.329 --> 00:04:33.631
He experimented long into the night.

54
00:04:34.472 --> 00:04:39.356
He often took short naps with his head on his desk so that he could continue to work.

55
00:04:40.877 --> 00:04:47.197
Thomas filled hundreds of books with notes, and at one time, said that he had considered over three

56
00:04:47.317 --> 00:04:50.558
thousand theories for creating light from electricity.

57
00:04:51.718 --> 00:04:53.559
Finally he had a breakthrough.

58
00:04:54.459 --> 00:05:00.001
He found that he could pass electricity through a cotton thread placed inside a glass bulb.

59
00:05:00.761 --> 00:05:07.103
While other inventors had worked on light-bulb designs, Edison was the first to create one that lasted for

60
00:05:07.143 --> 00:05:12.324
a long time and was cheap enough to be easily mass-produced.

61
00:05:12.344 --> 00:05:16.607
He installed his version of the electric light in his house in Menlo Park.

62
00:05:18.248 --> 00:05:25.033
People from all over flocked to see Edison's new invention, and Thomas became an overnight celebrity.

63
00:05:25.793 --> 00:05:28.775
People began calling him the Wizard of Menlo Park.

64
00:05:29.756 --> 00:05:35.020
Soon towns all over the world lit up their houses and streets with electric lights.

65
00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:41.344
Even though Thomas now had enough money to retire, he continued to work on new inventions.

66
00:05:42.676 --> 00:05:49.420
He invented a Kinetoscope, a light box that showed moving pictures, which later became the movie camera.

67
00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:57.884
He also worked on developing an electric car, electric batteries, a cement mixer, and a copying machine.

68
00:05:58.865 --> 00:06:07.049
In his lifetime Thomas Edison held 1,093 patents and would have continued to invent if he had not

69
00:06:07.109 --> 00:06:07.790
fallen sick.

70
00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:13.458
He died on October 18, 1931, at the age of 84.

71
00:06:14.178 --> 00:06:19.163
In his honor the lights all over the United States were turned off on the night of his

72
00:06:19.203 --> 00:06:22.946
funeral. Thomas Edison left a great legacy.

73
00:06:24.027 --> 00:06:28.611
Our everyday life would not be the same if it were not for his curious mind.

74
00:06:29.452 --> 00:06:33.575
His inventions are still used today by many people on the planet.
